<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elba is the largest island of the Tuscan Archipelago, off the coast of Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, facing Piombino. Its iron ore was mined from Etruscan times, when it was smelted at Populonia, until the 20th century. The island belonged to the lords of Piombino and partly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was exiled there as its sovereign from 1814 to 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elba.jpg" alt="Elba" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mjobling · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Elba_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The copper and iron of the Colline Metallifere and the iron of <b>Elba</b> made the coastal cities exceptionally rich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>Elba</b> Cosimo founded the fortified port of Portoferraio, originally named Cosmopoli, in 1548, and Livorno was enclosed by modern bastions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek traders and settlers, first at Pithekoussai on the island of Ischia and then at Cumae, came for the copper and iron of Etruria’s hills and of the island of <b>Elba</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The allied powers signed the treaty confirming Napoleon's abdication and granting him the island of <b>Elba</b> as a sovereign principality.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A de Havilland Comet, the world's first jet airliner, disintegrated near <b>Elba</b>, killing all 35 aboard.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Populonia, Etruscan Pupluna or Fufluna, stood on the Piombino promontory facing <b>Elba</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/populonia">Populonia</a></li></ul></section>
